meta {
  name: Upload Transfer Document
  type: http
  seq: 7
}

put {
  url: {{transferDocumentUploadUrl}}
  body: text
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/pdf
}

body:text {
  %PDF-1.4
  1 0 obj<</Type/Catalog>>endobj
  trailer<</Root 1 0 R>>
  %%EOF
}

tests {
  test("storage accepts the upload", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });
}

docs {
  # PUT <uploadUrl> — the raw New Business Application upload

  Not an API endpoint: this goes **straight to object storage** using the URL
  from `Presign Transfer Document`, so the file bytes never pass through the
  API.

  - **Auth:** `none` — deliberately. The collection-level bearer would be sent
    on this request and **break the S3 signature**; a presigned URL carries its
    own credentials.

  ## Why the collection cannot skip this step

  `Record Policy Transfer` runs the sold-deal intake pipeline, and
  `SoldSubmissionValidator.verifyAttachments` does two independent checks on
  every declared attachment: `assertKeyOwnership` (a key from another agency or
  another purpose prefix is refused before storage is touched at all) and then
  `statObject`. A key that was presigned but never written is a
  `404 An uploaded document was not found in storage.` — so a fixture that
  presigned and moved on would fail the transfer for a reason that looks
  nothing like the cause.
}
